**TL;DR:** after years of not knowing why my laptops would have worse performance than I thought
they should, coding agents helped me finally figure out that Windows apps are broken (like the Razer
apps or the NVIDIA overlay on your PC right now), but a few scripts and adjustments can fix them
ad hoc! A feeling I never thought I would have.

Growing up, my friends and I couldn't afford any gaming computers, and having a laptop and a desktop
was out of the question, so we always had to milk the last ounce of performance from whatever we
had. Countless videos watched and guides read to get the NVIDIA Control Panel settings as good as we
knew how, learning what each setting in the game did to find the most "value for buck" setup, etc.
As time passed, things like the GeForce app started automating these, and now that I can afford
(well, maybe could, a few years ago before the RAM prices) a desktop, I only get to use it once a
month, being so far away from everyone. But I still have a personal laptop, a Legion with a 3070
laptop GPU, which is good enough for my occasional "hop on Discord to play Peak or Subnautica 2 or
anything" with friends.

Today was such a day. We found time, booted up the game, and... 2 FPS?

Normally I would have maybe gone for a restart, but since I started using this computer as a
server, restarting is a huge hassle, as I have to make sure all the services come back up etc. Worse,
my wife was already using an app hosted on this computer, and I didn't want to cut her flow off. So
with Opus 5.5 at hand, I wanted to finally figure out what was going on. I was previously burned by
memory leaks in `audiodg.exe` from Nahimic, so a memory leak, in addition to plain old thermal
throttling, were the first culprits in my mind. However, I had just never learned how to diagnose
these issues properly.

## Rounding up the usual suspects

The nice thing about doing this with an agent is that every hunch turns into a command, and every
command either backs the hunch up or kills it, and you don't have to know the incantations yourself.
So we went down my list, and then some:

<SuspectList suspects={[
  { name: "Another app on the same GPU", evidence: "real: one of the services I host on this machine was using the GPU way more than it should. Moved it off. 2 → 30 fps, but still stuttering", guilty: false },
  { name: "Thermals", evidence: "CPU at 94 °C, but still boosting to 3.9 GHz. Not throttling", guilty: false },
  { name: "NVIDIA Overlay leaking memory", evidence: "real: one overlay process from 20 days ago held 12.7 GB, with commit at 48.6 of 49.9 GB. Killed it. Stutter unchanged", guilty: false },
  { name: "Frame pacing, G-Sync", evidence: "turned G-Sync on. Still stuttering, and now I noticed the terminal was too", guilty: false },
  { name: "Something inside the Windows kernel", evidence: "the System process was burning 1.1–1.4 cores nonstop", guilty: true },
]} />

So my memory leak hunch was right, just not about the app I expected. It wasn't Nahimic this time,
it was the NVIDIA overlay, sitting on 12.7 GB for 20 days (the exact kind of thing a restart would
have quietly wiped away without me ever knowing). Killing it felt great, and changed nothing.

The thing that actually mattered was almost an accident. After turning G-Sync on I noticed the
terminal I was typing into was stuttering too. A terminal! That is not a game fighting for GPU
memory, so whatever this was sat below everything, somewhere in Windows itself, and that is a place
I had honestly never looked before.

## Going into the kernel

This is the part I would have never done alone. Opus suggested a 10 second kernel trace with call
stacks (xperf, run as admin), to see what the System process was actually spending its time on:

```
dxgmms2.sys!VidMmWorkerThreadProc              75.81%
  dxgmms2.sys!VIDMM_GLOBAL::HandleTrimWnf      75.28%
    ntkrnlmp.exe!NtUpdateWnfStateData          74.68%
      ntkrnlmp.exe!ExpWnfFindScopeInstance     74.30%   (57.9% exclusive)
ntkrnlmp.exe!memcmp                            15.97%   (exclusive)
```

I could read maybe half of those words at first, so here is the version I wish someone had given me,
about how Windows draws things and how this stalls all of it:

<StallChain title="how a dead program stalls your screen" steps={[
  { plain: "Every window, your game and your terminal included, renders into GPU memory. The Desktop Window Manager (DWM) composites those surfaces into the frame you see.", kernel: "dwm.exe, dxgkrnl.sys" },
  { plain: "GPU memory is shared, so one part of the kernel manages it for everyone: the video memory manager, VidMm.", kernel: "dxgmms2.sys!VidMmWorkerThreadProc" },
  { plain: "When memory gets tight (a game using 7 of 8 GB will do it), VidMm evicts allocations and notifies processes so they can shrink.", kernel: "VIDMM_GLOBAL::HandleTrimWnf" },
  { plain: "That notification goes through WNF, the kernel's internal pub/sub. Before it can publish, the kernel has to find the right scope instance, and it does that by walking a list and comparing entries one by one.", kernel: "ExpWnfFindScopeInstance + memcmp", stat: "74% of System CPU" },
  { plain: "The list was bloated by processes that had exited but never been cleaned up.", kernel: "33,015 process objects, 306 alive", stat: "32,709 zombies" },
  { plain: "While VidMm's worker is busy walking that list, every app waiting on GPU memory waits too, and that includes DWM. So the whole screen hitches, not just the game.", kernel: "System process", stat: "~1.25 cores, nonstop" },
]} />

## 32,709 processes that don't exist

When a program exits but something still holds a handle to it, Windows can't free it, and it sticks
around as a zombie. Comparing the kernel's process object count with the processes actually running
gave me this:

```
Live processes: 306   Process objects in kernel: 33,015   => zombies ~ 32,709
```

Thirty two thousand. On a laptop that was running maybe three hundred things.

The top handle holders were Razer Game Manager (33,695 handles) and Razer Synapse Service (28,193).
Game Manager watches every process you launch so it can tell when you start a game, and it looks
like it opens a handle to each one and just never closes it. Over the 20 days since my last restart
that's about 1,600 new zombies a day, and every single one makes that list a little longer.

Drag the slider back to zero to see what a restart does:

<LinearScan />

A linear search is totally fine when the list is short, and fresh after a reboot it is. By day 20
every lookup is about 100 times more expensive, and it happens every time a game pushes on GPU
memory. Which is why the slowdown creeps up over weeks, and why a restart "fixes" it, and why I spent
years thinking my laptop just got tired sometimes.

So I stopped both Razer services, and within a minute the zombies dropped from 32,709 to 260, and the
System process went from about 1.25 cores to 0.2. The stutter was gone. No restart, and nobody's flow
got cut off.

To be fair about what's proven here: the stack and the before and after numbers are measured. That
the zombies are what bloats this particular list is my reading of the evidence. They dropped together,
at the same moment and by about the same factor, but I haven't seen the Windows source, so take that
part with a grain of salt.

## Check yours

This is the part I actually wanted to bring back to my friends (and you). Open PowerShell and run:

```powershell
(Get-Counter '\Objects\Processes').CounterSamples[0].CookedValue - (Get-Process).Count
```

A few hundred is normal. If you see thousands, something is leaking, and this will usually tell you
who:

```powershell
Get-Process | Sort HandleCount -Desc | Select -First 5 Name, HandleCount
```

I'd love to know what other people find, so post your number and your top offender in the comments.

## The restart was hiding things

All those years of guides and control panel settings, and the thing eating my frames was never a
setting. It was the apps that are supposed to help, the mouse software and the overlay, slowly
breaking in the background, and me clearing it all away with a restart every time without ever
knowing which one did it. Restarting is a great fix and a terrible diagnosis. It clears every leak at
once, so you never learn who leaked, and the problem just builds back up.

Not being able to restart ended up being the best thing that could have happened to this laptop. My
gaming script now disables Razer's services before a game and warns me if the zombie count starts
climbing again, and I keep wondering how many of the restarts I've done over the years were hiding
something like this.
